  "status_text": "Two gates that flip bits can do the job. Make every light stay off once it goes out, and the minimum can grow without limit. Lean checked this for one light per t-point subset of 3t minus 1 points, t positive. A light must be on if its set misses every chosen set and off if its own set is chosen. Use AND, OR and NOT gates with at most two input wires each. With n subsets, the minimum is two NOTs, attained in at most n squared plus 5n plus 2 total gates. When lights stay off as choices grow, b NOTs must satisfy 2^b > 1 + ceil((t+1)/2) (L-lib-1276).",
